8097255a-5b2d-4eec-bb8b-adff101609d3	2d624f01-55dd-4335-b315-83628796a4c0	assistant	A **t-test** is a statistical test used to determine whether a sample mean, or the difference between means, is statistically significant.\n\nFrom the document, two examples are shown:\n\n1. **Paired t-test**  \n   This is used when measurements are taken on the **same subjects before and after a treatment**. For example, measuring sales before and after an advertising strategy [Source 1].  \n   The hypotheses are:  \n   - **H₀:** μPOST = μPRE  \n   - **H₁:** μPOST ≠ μPRE  \n   meaning the test checks whether the average “after” value is different from the average “before” value [Source 1].\n\n2. **One-sample t-test**  \n   This is used to test whether the mean of a sample is significantly different from a known or hypothesized value. The document gives an example comparing participants’ average time to a value of 8891.37 seconds [Source 3]. In that example, the t statistic was **13.6138** and the p-value was **< .0001**, leading to rejection of the null hypothesis [Source 5].\n\nIn simple terms:  \nA **t-test helps you decide whether an observed difference in averages is likely real or could have happened by random chance**.\n\nCommon assumptions include that the subjects are randomly selected and, for paired t-tests, that the distribution of the sample mean differences is normal; for large samples, the central limit theorem can help justify this assumption [Source 1].	[{"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 428, "content": "C.1  Paired t-Tests C-3 \\nC.1 Paired t-Tests \\n3\\nPaired Samples\\nSales After\\nSales Before\\nAdvertising\\n \\nFor many types of data, repeat measurements are taken on the same subject throughout a study. The \\nsimplest form of this study is often referred to as the paired t-test. \\nIn this study design, \\n\\u2022 sub", "score": 0.38431909680366516}, {"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 337, "content": "2)   What is the confidence interval? \\n3)   How do you interpret this interval? \\n3.   Performing a One-Sample t-Test \\na.   Perform a one-sample t-test to determine whether the mean of the random sample of participants \\nin the data set sasuser.b_boston is significantly different from the average time", "score": 0.06218184903264046}, {"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 350, "content": "A-16 Appendix A  Exercises and Solutions \\n3.   Performing a One-Sample t-Test \\na.   Use PROC UNIV ARIATE to perform a one-sample t-test. \\nproc univariate data=sasuser.b_boston mu0=8891.37; \\n   var tottime; \\nrun; \\nPartial PROC UNIV ARIATE Output \\n                      Tests for Location: Mu0=8891.4 \\n", "score": 0.045888546854257584}]	2026-11-08 05:33:11.97128+00
