d0249441-ba4c-4227-a08d-dd86b54bf283	18825592-3594-4adc-bfbc-d2b524169974	assistant	Regression analysis is a statistical method used to study the relationship between a **response/dependent variable** and one or more **predictor/independent variables**.\n\n## 1. Basic idea\n\nIn regression, you try to explain or predict the behavior of one variable using another variable or several other variables.\n\nFor example, in the document’s fitness example:\n\n- **Response variable:** `Oxygen_Consumption`\n- **Predictor variable:** `Performance`\n\nThe analyst wants to understand how well `Performance` explains variation in `Oxygen_Consumption` among club members [Source 3].\n\n## 2. Simple linear regression\n\n**Simple linear regression** uses one predictor variable to explain or predict one response variable.\n\nAccording to the document, the objectives of simple linear regression are to:\n\n1. Assess whether the predictor variable significantly explains the variability or behavior of the response variable.\n2. Predict values of the response variable from values of the predictor variable [Source 3].\n\nSo, if we model:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Oxygen\\_Consumption} = a + b(\\text{Performance})\n\\]\n\nthen we are asking:\n\n- Does `Performance` help explain `Oxygen_Consumption`?\n- If we know a person’s performance measure, can we predict their oxygen consumption?\n\nThe document notes that `Performance` had the highest correlation with oxygen consumption, so the analyst decided to run a simple linear regression of `Oxygen_Consumption` versus `Performance` [Source 3].\n\n## 3. Explanatory or analytical regression\n\nRegression can also be used for **explanatory analysis**, where the goal is not just prediction but understanding relationships.\n\nThe document says that in analytical or explanatory analysis, the focus is on understanding the relationship between the dependent variable and the independent variables. Because of this, the statistical significance, magnitudes, and signs of the coefficients are important [Source 1].\n\nThat means you care about questions like:\n\n- Is the predictor statistically significant?\n- Is the relationship positive or negative?\n- How large is the effect?\n- Which variables seem most important?\n\n## 4. Multiple regression\n\nThe document also introduces a broader analytic example where the analyst considers several possible predictors of `Oxygen_Consumption`, including:\n\n- `Performance`\n- `Runtime`\n- `Age`\n- `Weight`\n- `Run_Pulse`\n- `Rest_Pulse`\n- `Maximum_Pulse`\n\nThe analyst already knows from simple linear regression that `Performance` is important, but now wants to investigate whether other variables are also important in explaining oxygen consumption [Source 1].\n\nThis is an example of **multiple regression**, where there is still one response variable, but there are multiple predictors.\n\nA model might look like:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Oxygen\\_Consumption} = a + b_1(\\text{Performance}) + b_2(\\text{Age}) + b_3(\\text{Weight}) + \\cdots\n\\]\n\n## 5. Regression and variability\n\nRegression is partly about explaining variability in the response variable.\n\nThe document’s ANOVA table for simple linear regression separates variability into:\n\n- **Model variability:** variability explained by the regression model.\n- **Error variability:** variability not explained by the model.\n- **Corrected Total variability:** total variability in the data [Source 5].\n\nIn the example shown, the model has an F value of 85.22 and a p-value less than .0001 [Source 5]. This suggests that the regression model explains a statistically significant amount of variation in the response variable.\n\n## 6. Key terms\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Response variable | The outcome you are trying to explain or predict |\n| Predictor variable | A variable used to explain or predict the response |\n| Simple linear regression | Regression with one predictor |\n| Multiple regression | Regression with more than one predictor |\n| Coefficient | A number showing the direction and size of a predictor’s effect |\n| Statistical significance | Evidence that a predictor is meaningfully related to the response |\n| Error | The part of the response variability not explained by the model |\n\n## 7. In plain language\n\nRegression analysis helps answer questions like:\n\n> “Does this variable help explain that outcome?”\n\nor\n\n> “Can I predict this outcome using this information?”\n\nIn the fitness example, regression is used to understand whether measures like `Performance`, `Age`, `Weight`, and pulse variables help explain a person’s `Oxygen_Consumption` [Source 1].	[{"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 175, "content": "3-52 Chapter 3  Regression  \\n  \\n67\\nAnalytical or Explanatory Analysis\\nThe focus is on understanding the relationship between \\nthe dependent variable and the independent variables.\\nConsequently, the statistical significance of the \\ncoefficients is important as well as the magnitudes and \\nsigns of the", "score": 0.8950488567352295}, {"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 151, "content": "3-28 Chapter 3  Regression  \\n  \\n33\\nSimple Linear Regression Analysis\\nThe objectives of simple linear regression are to\\n\\u0084 assess the significance of the predictor variable in \\nexplaining the variability or behavior of the \\nresponse variable\\n\\u0084 predict the values of the response variable given \\nthe val", "score": 0.7572291493415833}, {"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 159, "content": "3-36 Chapter 3  Regression  \\n  \\nThe Analysis of Variance (ANOV A) table provides an analysis of the variability observed in the data and \\nthe variability explained by the regression line. \\n                                      Analysis of Variance \\n \\n                                             Sum ", "score": 0.35103341937065125}]	2026-11-08 05:45:39.983274+00
