2dd53b16-e757-4571-b0ae-118a9cb51e55	dc578a86-2afc-4c81-a296-fed5e45ba3aa	assistant	Regression analysis is a statistical method used to study and model the relationship between an outcome variable, often called **Y**, and one or more predictor variables, often called **X variables**.\n\nIn regression, a model is used to explain or predict values of **Y** based on the values of the **X** variables. The document describes regression as useful for applications such as **prediction**, **variable screening**, **model specification**, and **parameter estimation** [Source 5]. For prediction, the focus is on producing a model that best predicts future values of **Y** as a function of the **X** variables [Source 5].\n\nIn **simple linear regression**, the model uses one predictor variable and a regression line. The Analysis of Variance table then compares the variability explained by the regression model with the variability not explained by the model [Source 3]. The document also identifies both **simple linear regression** and **multiple regression** as topics within regression analysis [Source 1].\n\nSo, in simple terms: **regression analysis helps you understand or predict an outcome using one or more related variables.**	[{"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 124, "content": "Chapter 3 Regression \\n \\n3.1 Exploratory Data Analysis .............................................................................................3-2 \\n3.2 Simple Linear Regression ...........................................................................................3-27  \\n3.3 Concepts of Multi", "score": 0.52082359790802}, {"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 159, "content": "3-36 Chapter 3  Regression  \\n  \\nThe Analysis of Variance (ANOV A) table provides an analysis of the variability observed in the data and \\nthe variability explained by the regression line. \\n                                      Analysis of Variance \\n \\n                                             Sum ", "score": 0.4154939353466034}, {"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 174, "content": "ranging from simple to complex, you will focus on applications for analytical studies and predictive \\nmodeling. Other SAS procedures are better suited for analyzing experimental data. \\nThe distinction between using multiple regression for an analytic analysis and prediction modeling is \\nsomewhat art", "score": 0.40694230794906616}]	2026-11-08 05:50:32.283386+00
