aedd538e-be7b-4ddb-bb2b-fd4f3ea94193	331fa2e0-eb7f-4f86-85bd-510b41d71402	assistant	## Linear regression, in simple terms\n\n**Linear regression** is a method for describing and predicting the relationship between:\n\n- a **response variable** — the outcome you care about, often called **Y**\n- one or more **predictor variables** — variables used to explain or predict Y, often called **X** [Source 3]\n\nIn **simple linear regression**, there is one predictor variable:\n\n\\[\nY = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1X + \\varepsilon\n\\]\n\nWhere:\n\n- \\(\\beta_0\\) = the **intercept**, the predicted value of Y when X = 0\n- \\(\\beta_1\\) = the **slope**, how much Y is expected to change when X increases by 1 unit\n- \\(\\varepsilon\\) = random error, the part of Y not explained by X\n\n---\n\n## The main idea\n\nLinear regression tries to draw a **straight line** through a cloud of data points.\n\nFor example, suppose you want to predict exam score from hours studied.\n\n| Hours studied | Exam score |\n|---:|---:|\n| 1 | 55 |\n| 2 | 60 |\n| 3 | 68 |\n| 4 | 75 |\n| 5 | 82 |\n\nA linear regression model might produce a line like:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Exam score} = 48 + 7(\\text{Hours studied})\n\\]\n\nThat means:\n\n- If a student studies 0 hours, the predicted score is 48.\n- For each additional hour studied, the predicted score increases by 7 points.\n\nSo if someone studies 4 hours:\n\n\\[\n48 + 7(4) = 76\n\\]\n\nThe predicted score is **76**.\n\n---\n\n## Why it is called “linear”\n\nIt is called linear regression because it assumes the **mean of the response variable is linearly related to the predictor variable**. In other words, the model assumes that a straight line connects the average values of Y at different values of X [Source 1].\n\nSo the model is not saying every point must fall exactly on a line. Instead, it says the **average trend** follows a line.\n\n---\n\n## What linear regression is used for\n\nLinear regression is used to:\n\n1. **Describe relationships**  \n   Example: Does income tend to increase with years of education?\n\n2. **Make predictions**  \n   Example: Predict house price based on square footage.\n\n3. **Test whether a predictor matters**  \n   The model can test whether the slope is different from 0. If the slope is not 0, then the predictor explains a meaningful amount of variation in the response variable [Source 1].\n\n---\n\n## Understanding the slope\n\nThe slope is usually the most important part.\n\nSuppose the model is:\n\n\\[\nY = 10 + 3X\n\\]\n\nThe slope is **3**.\n\nThat means:  \nFor every 1-unit increase in X, the predicted value of Y increases by 3.\n\nIf the model is:\n\n\\[\nY = 10 - 3X\n\\]\n\nThe slope is **-3**.\n\nThat means:  \nFor every 1-unit increase in X, the predicted value of Y decreases by 3.\n\nIf the slope is 0:\n\n\\[\nY = 10 + 0X\n\\]\n\nThen X does not help explain Y.\n\n---\n\n## How the best line is chosen\n\nA common method for fitting a linear regression line is **ordinary least squares**, or **OLS**. OLS chooses the line that minimizes the total squared differences between the observed values and the predicted values [Source 5].\n\nThe differences between actual and predicted values are called **residuals** or **errors**.\n\nFor each data point:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Residual} = \\text{Actual Y} - \\text{Predicted Y}\n\\]\n\nOLS tries to make these residuals as small as possible overall.\n\n---\n\n## Simple vs. multiple linear regression\n\n### Simple linear regression\n\nOne predictor:\n\n\\[\nY = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1X\n\\]\n\nExample:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Weight} = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1(\\text{Height})\n\\]\n\n### Multiple linear regression\n\nMore than one predictor:\n\n\\[\nY = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1X_1 + \\beta_2X_2 + \\cdots + \\beta_pX_p\n\\]\n\nExample:\n\n\\[\n\\text{House price} = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1(\\text{Size}) + \\beta_2(\\text{Bedrooms}) + \\beta_3(\\text{Age})\n\\]\n\nThe retrieved document specifically discusses **simple linear regression**, where one predictor is used to explain variability in one response variable [Source 3].\n\n---\n\n## Assumptions of linear regression\n\nThe document gives these assumptions for simple linear regression:\n\n1. **Linearity**  \n   The mean of the response variable is linearly related to the predictor variable [Source 1].\n\n2. **Normally distributed errors**  \n   The error terms should be normally distributed [Source 1].\n\n3. **Equal variances**  \n   The error terms should have equal variances at each value of the predictor variable [Source 1].\n\n4. **Independence**  \n   The errors should be independent at each value of the predictor variable [Source 1].\n\nAnother source also notes that linear regression generally requires predictors to be numeric, preferably continuous, and that missing values must be handled before fitting the model [Source 5].\n\n---\n\n## A quick intuition\n\nImagine throwing darts at a wall. The dots are your data points.\n\nLinear regression asks:\n\n> “Can I draw one straight line that summarizes the overall pattern of these dots?”\n\nIf the dots rise as X increases, the slope is positive.  \nIf the dots fall as X increases, the slope is negative.  \nIf the dots show no clear trend, the slope may be close to zero.\n\n---\n\n## In one sentence\n\n**Linear regression is a statistical method that uses a straight line to explain or predict a response variable from one or more predictor variables.**	[{"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 156, "content": "variability in the response variable. \\nIf the estimated simple linear regression model does fit the data better than the baseline model, you reject \\nthe null hypothesis. Thus, you do have enough evidence to say that the slope of the regression line in the \\npopulation is not 0 and that the predictor ", "score": 0.7253807783126831}, {"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 150, "content": "3.2  Simple Linear Regression 3-27  \\n \\n3.2 Simple Linear Regression \\n31\\nObjectives\\n\\u0084 Explain the concepts of simple linear regression.\\n\\u0084 Fit a simple linear regression using\\nthe REG procedure.\\n\\u0084 Produce predicted values and confidence intervals.\\n \\n32\\nOverview\\nY\\nX\\n* ** * * * * ** * * * * * ** ** * * ", "score": 0.7153499722480774}, {"document_name": "Data Preparation for Data Mining Using SAS.pdf", "page_number": 41, "content": "Equation 3.1 to the training portion of the mining view. There are several methods to\\n\\ufb01t the data to the form of 3.1. The most common is known as ordinary least squares\\n(OLS). PROC REG of SAS/STAT implements OLS to \\ufb01t linear regression models. It also\\nprovides a comprehensive set of diagnostic measu", "score": 0.6131018400192261}]	2026-11-08 06:32:12.921424+00
