ed053026-75aa-4003-8658-49493115ff0d	b23794fb-c1ba-4303-b309-535e8a171cf6	assistant	Hypothesis testing is a statistical process used to decide whether evidence from data is strong enough to reject an initial assumption, called the **null hypothesis**.\n\nIn general:\n\n1. **Set hypotheses**\n   - The **null hypothesis** \\(H_0\\) is the starting assumption, usually meaning equality or no relationship.\n   - The **alternative hypothesis** \\(H_1\\) is the research idea, usually meaning there is a difference or relationship. [Source 5]\n\n2. **Choose a significance level**\n   - The significance level, often written as \\(\\alpha\\), represents the Type I error rate and is the amount of evidence needed to reject \\(H_0\\). [Source 5]\n\n3. **Collect data and calculate evidence**\n   - The evidence is often summarized using a **p-value**. [Source 5]\n\n4. **Make a decision**\n   - If the p-value is **less than** \\(\\alpha\\), reject the null hypothesis.\n   - If the p-value is **greater than or equal to** \\(\\alpha\\), fail to reject the null hypothesis. [Source 5]\n\nA useful analogy is a court trial: the null hypothesis is like assuming the defendant is “not guilty,” and only strong enough evidence leads you to reject that assumption. Failing to reject the null does **not** prove it is true; it only means the evidence was not strong enough. [Source 3]\n\nSo, hypothesis testing helps answer questions like:  \n**“Is the observed result likely due to random chance, or is there enough evidence to suggest a real difference or effect?”**	[{"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 57, "content": "1.4  Hypothesis Testing 1-49  \\n \\n\\u2022 This statistic measures how far x  is from the hypothesized mean. \\n\\u2022 To reject a test with this statistic, the t statistic should be much higher or lower than 0 and have a small \\ncorresponding p-value. \\n\\u2022 The results of this test are valid if the distribution of