6827dee4-4f48-4f67-85fd-295d23902544	a1b2c3d4-e5f6-4789-8123-456789abcdef	assistant	Based on the available evidence, **the limitations most reducible through better data preparation are the data-quality and input-variable problems**. The retrieved sources for this follow-up do not fully restate every previously identified limitation, so the mapping below is limited to what the documents support.\n\n| Limitation | Can better data preparation reduce it? | How |\n|---|---:|---|\n| **Missing values** | **Yes** | Missing values should be replaced with reasonable values; missing-indicator variables may be added when missingness is related to the target [Source 5]. |\n| **Poor data quality reflected by missingness** | **Partly** | The documents warn that missingness may reflect poor data quality, making such predictors less likely to generalize well. Better profiling, validation, and cleaning can reduce this risk, though it may not fully fix source-system quality issues [Source 1]. |\n| **High-cardinality nominal variables** | **Yes** | Nominal variables with many levels should be collapsed to reduce quasi-complete separation and redundancy among levels [Source 5]. |\n| **Redundant predictors / too many input variables** | **Yes** | Variable clustering can reduce redundancy among numerous input variables [Source 5]. |\n| **Weak predictors with low or no predictive power** | **Yes** | Data preparation can remove independent variables that have low or no predictive power [Source 3]. |\n| **Model performance problems caused by preparation order or source-data structure** | **Potentially** | The order of preparation steps can be altered to improve performance and accommodate the data warehouse or source data; some operations can be done before integration and may improve model performance [Source 3]. |\n| **Difficulty identifying nonlinear associations** | **Partly** | Hoeffding’s D can help identify nonlinear associations during input preparation, although detecting them does not by itself fully solve modeling challenges [Source 5]. |\n\n## Not clearly reducible through data preparation alone\n\nFor broader limitations previously discussed—such as **general model complexity, interpretability, or the need for expert judgment**—the retrieved evidence does **not** show that data preparation alone resolves them. In fact, the documents indicate that subject-matter considerations still play a role in selecting variables and interpreting missingness patterns [Source 1].\n\n**Bottom line:** better data preparation can most directly reduce limitations involving **missing data, poor-quality inputs, high-cardinality categories, redundant variables, weak predictors, and some performance issues**. It can help with nonlinear-pattern detection, but it does not fully eliminate higher-level modeling challenges.	[{"document_name": "Statistics -II Regression_for_Predictive_Modeling_CourseNotes.pdf", "page_number": 86, "content": "3-38 Chapter 3  Preparing the Input Variables  \\n  \\n \\nTo reduce the amount of text that needs to be entered, a new macro variable named Reduced is created \\nthat contains the names of all the variables selected from PROC V ARCLUS. As was mentioned above, \\nsubject-matter considerations should play a pa", "score": 0.5032572746276855}, {"document_name": "Data Preparation for Data Mining Using SAS.pdf", "page_number": 38, "content": "operation can also be performed on each table extracted from the data\\nwarehouse before the integration of the data. This can sometimes result in\\nimproved model performance.\\n5. Removing independent variables that have low or no predictive power.\\nThis order of steps may be altered to improve performan", "score": 0.42460963129997253}, {"document_name": "Statistics -II Regression_for_Predictive_Modeling_CourseNotes.pdf", "page_number": 118, "content": "3-70 Chapter 3  Preparing the Input Variables  \\n  \\n3.5 Chapter Summary \\nPreparing the data for predictive modeling can be laborious. First, missing values need to be replaced with \\nreasonable values. Missing indicator variables are also needed if missingness is related to the target. If \\nthere are n", "score": 0.2512417733669281}]	2026-11-09 10:42:43.981225+00
