d4769394-a16e-458c-a5a8-b8cfba76cc76	466ac00e-200f-4d3d-b0fc-44e7ec19f950	assistant	Linear and logistic regression are both regression methods, but they are used for different kinds of outcomes and are estimated differently.\n\n| Aspect | Linear regression | Logistic regression |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Outcome variable** | Used for a continuous, often normally distributed response. | Used for a **binary outcome variable**, such as yes/no or 0/1 [Source 1]. |\n| **What it models** | Models the response directly as a linear function of predictors, with residual/error terms. | Models the **logit** of the probability of the binary outcome, rather than the outcome directly [Source 1]. |\n| **Distribution/variance assumptions** | Commonly associated with normally distributed errors and constant variance. | The logit is **not normally distributed**, and the variance is **not constant** [Source 1]. |\n| **Estimation method** | Typically simpler to estimate than logistic regression. | Usually estimated by **maximum likelihood**, which finds parameter values most likely to have produced the observed data [Source 1]. |\n| **Computation** | Generally less computationally expensive. | More computationally expensive because estimation often requires iterative optimization or solving nonlinear equations [Source 2], [Source 3]. |\n| **Runtime on large data** | Usually faster for equivalent problems. | Can take longer on large training datasets with many variables [Source 3]. |\n\nIn short: **linear regression is mainly for predicting continuous values**, while **logistic regression is mainly for predicting probabilities/classes for binary outcomes**. Logistic regression uses a nonlinear link function and maximum-likelihood estimation, making it generally more computationally intensive than linear regression [Source 1], [Source 2], [Source 3].	[{"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 289, "content": "\\u03b21 slope of the regression line\\n\\u03b5i error (residual) associated with each \\nobservation.\\n \\nFor a binary outcome variable, the linear logistic model with one predictor variable has the form above. \\nUnlike linear regression, the logit is not normally distributed and the variance is not constant. Also, \\n", "score": 0.9996647834777832}, {"document_name": "Statistics -II Regression_for_Predictive_Modeling_CourseNotes.pdf", "page_number": 26, "content": "linear regression on a normally distributed response. They must be determined using an iterative \\noptimization algorithm. Consequently, logistic regression is considerably more computationally expensive \\nthan linear regression. \\nSoftware for ML estimation of the logistic model is commonplace. Many S", "score": 0.9994113445281982}, {"document_name": "Data Preparation for Data Mining Using SAS.pdf", "page_number": 44, "content": "the model parameters. Furthermore, because the model equations involve nonlin-\\near link functions, the estimation procedure involves the iterative solution of a set of\\nnonlinear equations. Therefore, for tasks involving large training datasets with a large\\nnumber of variables, logistic regression ta", "score": 0.9954525828361511}]	2026-11-12 10:21:16.115912+00
