f6893db0-34ad-435e-9b2c-44cd08dce463	7ef75f7a-a128-4371-84b5-83488f8efd8a	assistant	The basic formula for **simple linear regression** is:\n\n\\[\nY = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1X + \\varepsilon\n\\]\n\nWhere:\n\n- \\(Y\\) = response/dependent variable  \n- \\(X\\) = predictor/independent variable  \n- \\(\\beta_0\\) = intercept, the expected value of \\(Y\\) when \\(X = 0\\)  \n- \\(\\beta_1\\) = slope, the expected change in \\(Y\\) for a one-unit increase in \\(X\\)  \n- \\(\\varepsilon\\) = error term, representing deviations from the regression line [Source 1]\n\nIn practice, after estimating the parameters from data, the fitted regression equation is usually written as:\n\n\\[\n\\hat{Y} = b_0 + b_1X\n\\]\n\nFor example, one cited regression equation is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Predicted Oxygen Consumption} = 35.58 + 1.48(\\text{Performance})\n\\]\n\nThis means that each one-unit increase in Performance is associated with a predicted increase of 1.48 units in Oxygen Consumption [Source 2].	[{"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 152, "content": "3.2  Simple Linear Regression 3-29  \\n \\n35\\nSimple Linear Regression Model\\n1 unit\\nunits\\nResponse (Y)\\nPredictor (X)\\n \\nThe relationship between the response variable and the predictor variable can be characterized by the \\nequation Y = \\u03b20 + \\u03b21X + \\u03b5 \\nwhere \\nY response variable \\nX predictor variable \\n\\u03b20 in", "score": 0.9556509256362915}, {"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 161, "content": "the response variable. \\nBecause the estimate of \\u03b2o=35.58 and \\u03b21=1.48, the estimated regression equation is given by \\nPredicted Oxygen_Consumption = 35.58 + 1.48(Performance). \\nThe model indicates that an increase of one unit for Performance amounts to a 1.48 increase in \\nOxygen_Consumption. However,", "score": 0.9447131752967834}, {"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 159, "content": "3-36 Chapter 3  Regression  \\n  \\nThe Analysis of Variance (ANOV A) table provides an analysis of the variability observed in the data and \\nthe variability explained by the regression line. \\n                                      Analysis of Variance \\n \\n                                             Sum ", "score": 0.8510074019432068}]	2026-11-10 16:27:32.805746+00
