386445ba-1e48-4a91-965b-a36f500e8b02	13129ba6-6dfe-44b3-99af-0f61fad13e0d	assistant	## Regression analysis explained\n\nRegression analysis is a statistical method used to study and model the relationship between a **response variable** and one or more **predictor variables**.\n\nIn the simplest case, **simple linear regression**, the goal is to define a linear relationship between:\n\n- a **response variable**: the main variable you want to understand or predict; and\n- a **predictor variable**: the variable used to explain variation in the response variable [Source 3].\n\nFor example, a study might examine whether a person’s **performance** can be explained or predicted using their **oxygen consumption** level [Source 1].\n\n## Main objectives\n\nSimple linear regression has two main objectives:\n\n1. **Explain variation**  \n   It assesses whether the predictor variable significantly explains the variability or behavior of the response variable [Source 1].\n\n2. **Make predictions**  \n   It can be used to predict values of the response variable based on known values of the predictor variable [Source 1].\n\n## Basic idea\n\nA regression line is fitted through the data to describe the relationship between the predictor and response variables. In simple linear regression, the predictor values are treated as fixed, and the analysis focuses on explaining the variability in the response variable given those predictor values [Source 1].\n\nA typical simple linear regression model is:\n\n\\[\nY = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1X + \\varepsilon\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n\n- \\(Y\\) is the response variable,\n- \\(X\\) is the predictor variable,\n- \\(\\beta_0\\) is the intercept,\n- \\(\\beta_1\\) is the slope, showing how much \\(Y\\) changes for a one-unit change in \\(X\\),\n- \\(\\varepsilon\\) is the error term.\n\n## How model fit is assessed\n\nRegression analysis often uses an **Analysis of Variance**, or **ANOVA**, table to separate total variation in the data into:\n\n- **Model variation**: variability explained by the regression model;\n- **Error variation**: variability not explained by the model;\n- **Corrected total variation**: total variability in the data [Source 2].\n\nThe ANOVA table can also include an **F value** and associated probability value, which help assess whether the regression model explains a statistically significant amount of variation in the response variable [Source 2].\n\n## In short\n\nRegression analysis helps answer questions such as:\n\n- Is there a meaningful relationship between two variables?\n- How strong is that relationship?\n- Can one variable be used to predict another?\n- How much variation in the response variable is explained by the predictor?\n\nSimple linear regression is the basic form, involving one predictor and one response variable. More advanced forms, such as multiple regression, extend the same idea to several predictors.	[{"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 151, "content": "3-28 Chapter 3  Regression  \\n  \\n33\\nSimple Linear Regression Analysis\\nThe objectives of simple linear regression are to\\n\\u0084 assess the significance of the predictor variable in \\nexplaining the variability or behavior of the \\nresponse variable\\n\\u0084 predict the values of the response variable given \\nthe val", "score": 0.9923601746559143}, {"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 159, "content": "3-36 Chapter 3  Regression  \\n  \\nThe Analysis of Variance (ANOV A) table provides an analysis of the variability observed in the data and \\nthe variability explained by the regression line. \\n                                      Analysis of Variance \\n \\n                                             Sum ", "score": 0.9672630429267883}, {"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 150, "content": "3.2  Simple Linear Regression 3-27  \\n \\n3.2 Simple Linear Regression \\n31\\nObjectives\\n\\u0084 Explain the concepts of simple linear regression.\\n\\u0084 Fit a simple linear regression using\\nthe REG procedure.\\n\\u0084 Produce predicted values and confidence intervals.\\n \\n32\\nOverview\\nY\\nX\\n* ** * * * * ** * * * * * ** ** * * ", "score": 0.9577440619468689}]	2026-11-11 04:47:01.587957+00
