ab821a71-daf4-4e30-ac2e-3e198bee3758	961b015b-4822-470a-ae59-69e777ad0a72	assistant	## Regression analysis explained\n\nRegression analysis is a statistical method used to describe and quantify the relationship between a **response variable** and one or more **predictor variables**.\n\nIn **simple linear regression**, there is one predictor variable and one response variable. The goal is to define a linear relationship between them, often represented as:\n\n\\[\nY = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1X + \\varepsilon\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n\n- \\(Y\\) is the **response variable** — the outcome or variable of primary interest.\n- \\(X\\) is the **predictor variable** — the variable used to explain variation in \\(Y\\).\n- \\(\\beta_0\\) is the intercept.\n- \\(\\beta_1\\) is the slope, showing how much \\(Y\\) is expected to change when \\(X\\) increases by one unit.\n- \\(\\varepsilon\\) represents random error or unexplained variation.\n\nThe retrieved material describes the response variable as “the variable of primary interest” and the predictor variable as the variable used to explain variability in the response variable [Source 3].\n\n## Main objectives\n\nThe main objectives of simple linear regression are to:\n\n- **Assess whether the predictor variable is significant** in explaining the variability or behavior of the response variable.\n- **Predict values of the response variable** given values of the predictor variable [Source 1].\n\nFor example, if an analyst believes that a person’s performance is related to oxygen consumption, they might run a simple linear regression using **Performance** as the predictor and **Oxygen_Consumption** as the response to explore that relationship [Source 1].\n\n## How regression evaluates a model\n\nRegression analysis separates the total variation in the data into parts:\n\n- **Model variation**: the variability explained by the regression model.\n- **Error variation**: the variability not explained by the model.\n- **Total variation**: the overall variability in the data [Source 2].\n\nThis breakdown is often shown in an **Analysis of Variance**, or **ANOVA**, table. In regression, the ANOVA table helps evaluate how much variability is explained by the regression line compared with how much remains unexplained [Source 2].\n\n## Why regression is useful\n\nRegression analysis is useful because it allows you to:\n\n- Understand whether variables are related.\n- Estimate the strength and direction of that relationship.\n- Test whether a predictor is statistically meaningful.\n- Make predictions for new or existing predictor values.\n- Produce predicted values and confidence intervals [Source 3].\n\nIn short, regression analysis helps explain and predict outcomes by modeling how a response variable changes as predictor variables change.	[{"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 151, "content": "3-28 Chapter 3  Regression  \\n  \\n33\\nSimple Linear Regression Analysis\\nThe objectives of simple linear regression are to\\n\\u0084 assess the significance of the predictor variable in \\nexplaining the variability or behavior of the \\nresponse variable\\n\\u0084 predict the values of the response variable given \\nthe val", "score": 0.9923601746559143}, {"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 159, "content": "3-36 Chapter 3  Regression  \\n  \\nThe Analysis of Variance (ANOV A) table provides an analysis of the variability observed in the data and \\nthe variability explained by the regression line. \\n                                      Analysis of Variance \\n \\n                                             Sum ", "score": 0.9672630429267883}, {"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 150, "content": "3.2  Simple Linear Regression 3-27  \\n \\n3.2 Simple Linear Regression \\n31\\nObjectives\\n\\u0084 Explain the concepts of simple linear regression.\\n\\u0084 Fit a simple linear regression using\\nthe REG procedure.\\n\\u0084 Produce predicted values and confidence intervals.\\n \\n32\\nOverview\\nY\\nX\\n* ** * * * * ** * * * * * ** ** * * ", "score": 0.9577440619468689}]	2026-11-11 04:58:09.53972+00
