f26ea99c-5701-4cf2-a737-a17d29e8a317	0f1f9b8f-b825-4480-9aae-e8e0f1f56002	assistant	## Regression analysis\n\nRegression analysis is a statistical method used to study and model the relationship between a **response variable** and one or more **predictor variables**. The goal is to understand how changes in the predictor variable are associated with changes in the response variable, and to use that relationship for explanation or prediction.\n\n## Simple linear regression\n\nIn **simple linear regression**, there is:\n\n- One **response variable** — the main variable you want to explain or predict.\n- One **predictor variable** — the variable used to explain variation in the response variable.\n\nThe retrieved material defines simple linear regression as a way to “define the linear relationship between a response variable and a predictor variable” [Source 3]. The response variable is the variable of primary interest, while the predictor variable is used to explain variability in that response [Source 3].\n\nA typical simple linear regression model is:\n\n\\[\nY = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1X + \\varepsilon\n\\]\n\nWhere:\n\n- \\(Y\\) = response variable  \n- \\(X\\) = predictor variable  \n- \\(\\beta_0\\) = intercept  \n- \\(\\beta_1\\) = slope, or expected change in \\(Y\\) for a one-unit change in \\(X\\)  \n- \\(\\varepsilon\\) = random error term  \n\n## Main objectives\n\nAccording to the source material, the objectives of simple linear regression are to:\n\n1. **Assess the significance of the predictor variable** in explaining the variability or behavior of the response variable.\n2. **Predict values of the response variable** given values of the predictor variable [Source 1].\n\nIn other words, regression helps answer questions such as:\n\n- Is this predictor meaningfully related to the outcome?\n- How strong is the relationship?\n- What value of the response variable should we expect for a given predictor value?\n\n## Example\n\nThe document gives an example involving fitness data, where an analyst studies the relationship between **Performance** and **Oxygen_Consumption**. Because performance had a high correlation with oxygen consumption, the analyst used simple linear regression to further explore the relationship between the two variables [Source 1].\n\nThis illustrates a common use of regression: after identifying a possible relationship, regression is used to model it more formally.\n\n## Explained and unexplained variability\n\nRegression also separates the total variation in the data into parts:\n\n- **Model variability**: the variation explained by the regression model.\n- **Error variability**: the variation not explained by the model.\n- **Corrected total variability**: the total variability in the response data.\n\nThe source explains that an ANOVA table for regression analyzes both the variability observed in the data and the variability explained by the regression line [Source 2]. This helps determine whether the model explains a statistically meaningful amount of variation.\n\n## In summary\n\nRegression analysis is used to model relationships between variables, explain variation in an outcome, and make predictions. In simple linear regression, one predictor variable is used to explain or predict one response variable. The method evaluates whether the predictor is statistically meaningful and how much of the response variable’s variability is explained by the regression model.	[{"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 151, "content": "3-28 Chapter 3  Regression  \\n  \\n33\\nSimple Linear Regression Analysis\\nThe objectives of simple linear regression are to\\n\\u0084 assess the significance of the predictor variable in \\nexplaining the variability or behavior of the \\nresponse variable\\n\\u0084 predict the values of the response variable given \\nthe val", "score": 0.9923601746559143}, {"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 159, "content": "3-36 Chapter 3  Regression  \\n  \\nThe Analysis of Variance (ANOV A) table provides an analysis of the variability observed in the data and \\nthe variability explained by the regression line. \\n                                      Analysis of Variance \\n \\n                                             Sum ", "score": 0.9672630429267883}, {"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 150, "content": "3.2  Simple Linear Regression 3-27  \\n \\n3.2 Simple Linear Regression \\n31\\nObjectives\\n\\u0084 Explain the concepts of simple linear regression.\\n\\u0084 Fit a simple linear regression using\\nthe REG procedure.\\n\\u0084 Produce predicted values and confidence intervals.\\n \\n32\\nOverview\\nY\\nX\\n* ** * * * * ** * * * * * ** ** * * ", "score": 0.9577440619468689}]	2026-11-11 05:11:59.381533+00
