c344014d-0d49-4183-9c26-c29f7e941eee	df3767cb-33ba-45a5-a059-ba528912a13c	assistant	## Regression analysis, in brief\n\nRegression analysis is a statistical method used to describe and quantify the relationship between a **response variable**—the outcome of interest—and one or more **predictor variables**—variables used to explain or predict that outcome.\n\nIn **simple linear regression**, there is one predictor variable and one response variable. The goal is to define a linear relationship between them, often represented as a straight line through the data [Source 3].\n\n## Main objectives\n\nSimple linear regression has two main purposes:\n\n- **Explanation:** determine whether the predictor variable significantly explains variation in the response variable.\n- **Prediction:** estimate or predict values of the response variable for given values of the predictor variable [Source 1].\n\nFor example, a study might examine whether a person’s **performance** can be explained or predicted using their **oxygen consumption** level [Source 1].\n\n## Key concepts\n\n- **Response variable:** the variable you are trying to understand or predict.\n- **Predictor variable:** the variable used to explain changes or variability in the response variable [Source 3].\n- **Regression line:** the fitted line that summarizes the linear relationship between the predictor and response variables.\n- **Explained variability:** the portion of variation in the response variable accounted for by the regression model.\n- **Unexplained variability:** the remaining variation not accounted for by the model.\n\nIn simple linear regression, the predictor values are treated as fixed, and the analysis focuses on explaining the variability of the response variable given those predictor values [Source 1].\n\n## How model fit is assessed\n\nRegression often uses an **ANOVA table** to separate total variability in the data into:\n\n- **Model variability:** variation explained by the regression line.\n- **Error variability:** variation not explained by the regression model.\n- **Corrected total variability:** total variation observed in the data [Source 2].\n\nThe ANOVA table may also include statistics such as an **F value** and a **p-value**, which help assess whether the model explains a statistically significant amount of variation in the response variable [Source 2].\n\n## Summary\n\nRegression analysis helps answer questions such as:\n\n- Does a predictor meaningfully explain an outcome?\n- How strong is the relationship?\n- What value of the outcome should we expect for a given predictor value?\n- How much variation does the model explain?\n\nThe retrieved material specifically discusses **simple linear regression**, where one predictor is used to explain or predict one response variable. It does not provide detail on multiple regression, nonlinear regression, or logistic regression.	[{"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 151, "content": "3-28 Chapter 3  Regression  \\n  \\n33\\nSimple Linear Regression Analysis\\nThe objectives of simple linear regression are to\\n\\u0084 assess the significance of the predictor variable in \\nexplaining the variability or behavior of the \\nresponse variable\\n\\u0084 predict the values of the response variable given \\nthe val", "score": 0.9923601746559143}, {"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 159, "content": "3-36 Chapter 3  Regression  \\n  \\nThe Analysis of Variance (ANOV A) table provides an analysis of the variability observed in the data and \\nthe variability explained by the regression line. \\n                                      Analysis of Variance \\n \\n                                             Sum ", "score": 0.9672630429267883}, {"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 150, "content": "3.2  Simple Linear Regression 3-27  \\n