414d9d29-760f-46d7-b022-f414f5c72fa5	15854217-89dc-428d-97bf-939fc6e5ed47	assistant	## Difference between a t-test and ANOVA\n\nBoth **t-tests** and **ANOVA** are statistical methods used to compare **means**, but they are used in different situations.\n\n| Aspect | t-test | ANOVA |\n|---|---|---|\n| Main purpose | Compares the means of **two groups** | Compares means across **two or more groups**, especially useful for **three or more groups** |\n| Typical question | “Are these two group means different?” | “Is at least one group mean different from another?” |\n| Hypothesis | Tests whether two means differ | Tests **H₀: all means are equal** vs. **H₁: at least one mean is different** [Source 5] |\n| Test statistic | Uses a **t statistic** | Uses an **F statistic**; ANOVA output includes an F test for the overall model [Source 2] |\n| Interpretation | If significant, the two groups differ | If significant, it tells you that at least one group mean differs, but not necessarily which one [Source 1] |\n| Follow-up | Usually no follow-up needed when only two groups are compared | Often needs post-hoc or multiple comparison tests, such as Tukey, Bonferroni, or pairwise t-tests, to identify which groups differ [Source 3] |\n\n### Simple rule of thumb\n\n- Use a **t-test** when comparing the means of **two groups**.\n- Use **ANOVA** when comparing means across **three or more groups** or when your model includes multiple factors.\n- If ANOVA is statistically significant, it only shows that **some difference exists**; additional comparisons may be needed to find **where** the difference is [Source 1], [Source 3].\n\n### Example\n\nSuppose you want to compare average exam scores:\n\n- **Two teaching methods** → use a **t-test**.\n- **Three or more teaching methods** → use **ANOVA**.\n\nA key reason to use ANOVA instead of running many t-tests is that repeated pairwise tests can increase the chance of false positives; ANOVA and multiple-comparison methods help manage this issue [Source 3].	[{"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 93, "content": "2-32 Chapter 2  Analysis of Variance (ANOVA)  \\n  \\nAt this point, you know there is at least one treatment mean that is different from one other treatment \\nmean, but you cannot be sure which one(s) are different. Some insight can be gained by looking at the \\nside-by-side box plots from PROC UNIV ARIA", "score": 0.5265001654624939}, {"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 119, "content": "2-58 Chapter 2  Analysis of Variance (ANOVA)  \\n  \\nThe next part of the output, below, shows the source table with the F test for the overall model. This tests \\nthe null hypothesis that none of the effects in the model are statistically different, in other words, that \\nthere is no difference between ", "score": 0.14396242797374725}, {"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 95, "content": "2-34 Chapter 2  Analysis of Variance (ANOVA)  \\n  \\n43\\nMultiple Comparison Methods\\nControl\\nExperimentwise\\nError Rate\\nCompare All Pairs\\nTukey\\nP replanned Comparison\\nBonferroni\\nControl\\nComparisonwise\\nError Rate\\nPairwise t-tests\\n \\nAll of these multiple comparison methods are requested with options in the", "score": 0.1086200550198555}, {"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 72, "content": "2.1  One-Way ANOVA: Two Populations 2-11  \\n \\n17\\nPredicted and Residual Values\\nThe predicted value in ANOVA is the group mean.\\nA residual is the difference between the observed value of \\nthe response and the predicted value of the response \\nvariable.\\n \\nThe residuals from the ANOV A are calculated as ", "score": 0.09518624097108841}, {"document_name": "Statistics-I-Introduction-to-Anova-Regression-And-Logistic.pdf", "page_number": 86, "content": "Recall 